   "rule_summary": "A model’s failure rate must not exceed 7%. Exceeding this limit requires prompt notification to the regulator, a corrective-action and retesting plan, and any additional safeguards or sales restrictions required by the regulator based on demonstrated risk. Manufacturers must submit per-model results, sample size, and protocol to the regulator and provide the results to customers of that model in plain language.",
   "comparison": "weakened by: permanent_limit_pct, over_limit_consequence, transition",
   "final_rule_text": "Each model must undergo a sustained-heat test at 55 °C using a regulator-approved protocol and a representative sample before market entry, at least every two years thereafter, and after a material design change. A model’s failure rate must not exceed 7%. Exceeding this limit requires prompt notification to the regulator, a corrective-action and retesting plan, and any additional safeguards or sales restrictions required by the regulator based on demonstrated risk. Manufacturers must submit per-model results, sample size, and protocol to the regulator and provide the results to customers of that model in plain language. The limit must be reviewed after three years using test and incident evidence, with any change subject to joint regulatory review."
  },
